{
  "id": "dsm-025",
  "pattern": "data-structure-misuse",
  "difficulty_rating": 1800,
  "explanation": "A plain `{}` isn't an empty map — it already inherits every property of Object.prototype, including methods like `toString`. `index['toString']` was never assigned by buildIndex, but the lookup still finds the inherited function on the prototype chain, and that function is truthy, so `?? null` never kicks in for it. lookupOne('alpha') correctly returns the alpha record and lookupOne('gamma') correctly falls back to null, but lookupOne('toString') silently resolves to the inherited toString function instead of null, even though 'toString' was never registered as an id any more than 'gamma' was. Using `Object.create(null)` or a real `Map` for the index would avoid this entirely.",
  "prompt": "buildIndex uses a plain object as a lookup map. Step through lookupMany(records, ['alpha', 'toString', 'gamma']) and predict what it returns.",
  "language": "javascript",
  "snippet": "function buildIndex(records) {\n  const index = {};\n  for (const r of records) {\n    index[r.id] = r;\n  }\n  return index;\n}\nfunction lookupOne(records, id) {\n  const index = buildIndex(records);\n  return index[id] ?? null;\n}\nconst records = [{ id: 'alpha', name: 'first' }, { id: 'beta', name: 'second' }];\nconsole.log(lookupOne(records, 'alpha'));\nconsole.log(lookupOne(records, 'toString'));\nconsole.log(lookupOne(records, 'gamma'));",
